    {
      "text": "MODULAR CATEGORIES AND TQFTS BEYOND SEMISIMPLICITY — Vladimir Turaev discovered in the early years of quantum topol- ogy that the notion of modular category was an appropriate structure for build\n\n**MODULAR CATEGORIES AND TQFTS BEYOND SEMISIMPLICITY**\n\n*CHRISTIAN BLANCHET AND MARCO DERENZI*\n\n26 pp, 15 sections.\n\n**Abstract.** Vladimir Turaev discovered in the early years of quantum topol- ogy that the notion of modular category was an appropriate structure for build- ing 3-dimensional Topological Quantum Field Theories (TQFTs for short) con- taining invariants of links in 3-manifolds such as Witten–Reshetikhin–Turaev ones. In recent years, generalized notions of modular categories, which relax the semisimplicity requirement, have been successfully used to extend Turaev’s construction to various non-semisimple settings. We report on these recent de- velopments in the domain, showing the richness of Vladimir’s lineage.\n\n**Outline.** Front matter; 1 Introduction; 2 Historical overview; Definition of TQFT; Semisimple constructions; Early non-semisimple constructions; Renormalized non-semisimple constructions; 3 Turaev's notion of modular category; 4 Semisimple quantum invariants and TQFTs; 5 T-modular categories from small quantum sl(2; 6 Lyubashenko's notion of modular category; 7 Non-semisimple quantum invariants and TQFTs; 8 L-modular categories from small quantum sl(2; Appendix A. Ends; References",
      "lang": "en",
